document and release them.
}
\details{
The SMR sequence is a sandwich. Writing \code{n1} for the size of the first
block and \code{N} for the total sample size, the layout is

\preformatted{
  |<--- n1 --->|<-------- N - 2*n1 -------->|<--- n1 --->|
  +------------+----------------------------+------------+
  | block 1    | blocks 2 .. K              | block K+1  |
  | imbalanced | permuted blocks of         | complement |
  |            | varying sizes              | of block 1 |
  +------------+----------------------------+------------+
}

Block 1 and block K+1 are the two halves of one balanced pool of
\code{2*n1} slots, so the last block is the exact complement of the first
one: balance over the whole trial is guaranteed while the next allocation
stays unpredictable. Two further paths handle the boundary cases:
\code{N < 4 * ratio_sum} gives complete randomization in one single block
(path 1), and \code{N == 2 * n1} gives head and tail blocks only (path 3).

The head-block size \code{n1} is always sampled at random from the
admissible multiples of \code{ratio_sum} (there is no need to pre-specify
it and no user override is accepted), and the imbalance of the first block
is measured by the \emph{spread} of the deviations from the target
allocation, a generalisation that equals the between-group count difference
for a 1:1 allocation and that is zero for an exactly proportional block.
}
